<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Southbrook Production Media, security groups (Task C10).

  INTERNAL-ONLY access model. Everyday upload/QC access for ordinary
  internal users runs entirely through the ``production.media`` RPC
  surface (models/production_media.py): it enforces access on the
  *anchored business record* (product/MO/picking) via
  ``record.check_access("write")`` and then does its own DMS
  housekeeping under ``sudo()``, see the DEGRADE PATTERN docstring
  there. So ordinary internal users need no new grant on
  dms.directory/dms.file to use the QC panel; ``base.group_user``
  already has dms's own read-only model access from the ``dms`` module,
  and dms_field's per-record ``dms.access.group`` (auto-generated by
  ``dms.field.template.create_dms_directory()``) is the only source of
  *record-level* visibility for direct (non-RPC) DMS browsing, because
  our storage's ``save_type`` is "file"; dms_field forbids
  "attachment" storage on its templates (see
  ``dms_field/models/dms_field_template.py::storage_id`` domain), so
  the inheritance-based access path in
  ``dms.security.mixin._get_domain_by_inheritance()`` never applies
  here.

  ``Production Media Manager`` exists for the people who manage the raw
  DMS structure directly (re-parenting/renaming directories, deleting
  stale files) rather than through the RPC surface: granted full CRUD
  on dms.directory/dms.file in ir.model.access.csv alongside this
  group.

  Portal/public get nothing, here or upstream: dms/dms_field never add
  portal/public to any ``dms.access.group``, so even though dms's own
  ir.model.access.csv grants ``base.group_portal`` model-level read on
  dms.directory/dms.file, the record rules
  (dms/security/security.xml: rule_directory_computed_read etc.) still
  resolve to zero visible rows for them. ``production.media.upload_media()``
  additionally raises AccessError immediately for a portal user because
  it checks write access on the anchored business record first
  (mrp.production/product.template/stock.picking), none of which grant
  portal any access.

  ``implied_ids`` is set on THIS module's own new group record (not on
  ``base.group_user``'s own record), which is the working direction in
  v19: see memory odoo19_base_group_user_implied_ids_trap. The trap is
  specifically writing onto the shared ``base.group_user`` XML record
  from a downstream module (that silently no-ops); a new group implying
  ``base.group_user`` on its own record is the standard "is-a" pattern
  used elsewhere in this repo (southbrook_hermes, southbrook_kitchen_3d_configurator)
  and works fine.
-->
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">

        <record id="group_media_manager" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Production Media Manager</field>
            <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('base.group_user'))]"/>
            <field
                name="comment"
            >Manages the Production Media DMS structure directly (directories/files): full CRUD on dms.directory/dms.file. Ordinary internal users need no membership here; they use the Photos/Videos panel (production.media RPC), which is sudo()-mediated and gated on the anchored record's own write access.</field>
        </record>

    </data>
</odoo>
